Removal of Drain / Catheter
General Practice Other
Indwelling surgical drain or catheter, Minimal ongoing fluid output, Deflation of retention balloon/gentle extraction, Post-removal voiding monitoring

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe localized pain and functional immobility secondary to an accidentally snagged and partially displaced indwelling surgical drain.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient who had undergone a recent abdominal procedure, presenting with acute, severe pain after accidentally catching their Jackson-Pratt drain tubing on a door handle. The trauma partially pulled the drain from the surgical site, causing extreme localized pain and inability to mobilize. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to assess for active deep hemorrhage, safely completely remove the compromised drain (which had already ceased active output), and apply a sterile dressing. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in severe pain with an open, contaminated tract into the peritoneal cavity overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I caught the drain tube on a chair, it ripped halfway out of my stomach, and the sharp pain is making me feel sick."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the presence of an indwelling surgical drain following a recent open cholecystectomy performed 4 days ago. One hour ago, the patient sustained mechanical trauma to the tubing, causing partial avulsion. Prior to the trauma, there was a complete cessation or minimal volume of fluid output from the surgical drain over the last 24 hours. The patient reports a sudden, sharp, tearing sensation at the insertion site upon snagging the tube. There is an absence of localized signs of infection such as purulent discharge or severe erythema prior to the event. The pain is severe, sharply localized to the right upper quadrant insertion site, and exacerbated by any movement.
Functional Status: Currently immobile, guarding the abdomen heavily due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; fear of evisceration or severe internal damage from the pulled drain.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Distressed, holding the abdomen, leaning forward slightly.
Physical Exam: Abdominal: The primary surgical incision is clean, dry, and intact. The separate drain insertion site in the right upper quadrant shows the silicone tubing is partially withdrawn by approximately 4cm. The securing suture has been traumatically snapped. There is mild localized bleeding at the skin edge, but no massive hemorrhage, no purulent discharge, and no signs of bowel evisceration. The clinical presentation supports the gentle continuous traction allowing the smooth extraction of the remaining tubing, followed by the immediate application of a sterile dressing over the drain site. Expected investigation results include documentation of steadily decreasing fluid output in the preceding days (confirmed by patient log) preventing the need for further diagnostic investigation.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean environment; snag hazard (door handle) noted.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen verified; taken with zero relief since the trauma.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Removal of Drain / Catheter (Traumatically Displaced Jackson-Pratt Drain) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Intra-abdominal Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintained blood pressure, absence of severe generalized abdominal distension or rigid peritonitis, and only minor, localized capillary bleeding at the skin exit site.

Bowel Perforation / Evisceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No visible bowel tissue at the wound, no severe generalized sepsis, and the drain was placed superficially to the fascia.

Deep Surgical Site Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of purulent discharge, and no spreading erythema surrounding the exit wound prior to the mechanical trauma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient sustained a mechanical trauma partially avulsing a surgical drain, resulting in acute, severe localized pain and functional impairment. Telephone triage accurately identified that a partially displaced drain acts as an open, unsealed conduit for infection and causes immense physical distress. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to rule out massive internal bleeding or evisceration, and to cleanly and swiftly remove the remaining, non-functional foreign body. The extraction immediately resolved the sharp, tethering pain. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to unmanageable pain and a significantly high risk of deep space infection tracking down the compromised drain path overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Cleansed the insertion site with chlorhexidine. Released the vacuum suction on the bulb. Applied gentle, steady, continuous traction to smoothly extract the remaining 10cm of the silicone drain. Achieved immediate hemostasis with localized pressure. Applied a heavy, sterile, absorbent dressing over the open tract.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure the open drain tract heals cleanly without infection, commit to leaving the heavy dressing completely dry and entirely untouched for 48 hours. Place a waterproof plastic cover over it when showering. By avoiding the temptation to peel the dressing back to 'check' the wound, you actively prevent introducing environmental bacteria into the deep tissue tract, reinforcing hands-off, safe wound healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sterile dressings (provided from bag).
Follow-up: Routine GP or surgical outpatient review in 3 days for a wound check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if blood completely soaks through the dressing, if you develop a high fever with shivering, or if you develop sudden, severe, generalized abdominal pain that prevents you from standing up.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The sharp, pulling pain is completely gone now that the tube is out. The small hole will leak a small amount of clear or slightly bloody fluid into the dressing for the next 24 hours, which is normal as the tract closes.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the wound begins to drain thick, foul-smelling yellow or green pus tomorrow.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The drain had already done its job and was ready to come out. The accident simply pulled it halfway out, causing the severe sharp pain. Removing the rest of it cleanly allows the tract to close up and heal naturally.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing completely dry. Take Paracetamol tonight for the minor bruising ache. Do not lift anything heavy.

